The first critical issue to address is the Lock Expiry Race Condition. In a production environment with 600,000+ rows, the processing time can easily exceed the current 5-minute Redis TTL. If the lock expires while the background thread is still writing to Oracle, a user could accidentally trigger a second parallel upload for the same Request ID, leading to duplicate data.
Here are the specific code changes for JournalRequestServiceImpl.java.
File: JournalRequestServiceImpl.java
1. Update: createBatchFromCacheAsync
We will increase the TTL to 60 minutes. This provides a massive safety buffer for extremely large files or periods of high database latency.
Existing Code:
acquired = redisTemplate.opsForValue().setIfAbsent(lockKey, new byte[0], 5, TimeUnit.MINUTES);

Updated Code:
// Rule 3: Increased TTL to 60 minutes to account for high-volume (600k+) processing times
// that may exceed the previous 5-minute limit under heavy DB load.
acquired = redisTemplate.opsForValue().setIfAbsent(lockKey, new byte[0], 60, TimeUnit.MINUTES);

2. Update: executeAsyncBatchCreation
The lock deletion must be moved to the finally block. Currently, if the code hits an unexpected error before the try block finishes, the lock might stay in Redis until it expires, preventing the user from retrying. Moving it to finally ensures the lock is released as soon as the thread stops, regardless of success or failure.
Existing Code (Inside try and finally):
try {
    // ... logic ...
    redisTemplate.delete("LOCK_REQ_" + requestId); // Deletes here on success
    // ... logic ...
} catch (DataAccessException e) {
    handleAsyncFailure(batchId, creatorId, requestId, "Database persistence failed.");
} finally {
    MDC.clear();
}

Updated Code:
@Override
@Async("bulkExecutor")
@Transactional
public void executeAsyncBatchCreation(String batchId, String requestId, String commonRemarks, String creatorId, Integer creatorRole) {
    String lockKey = "LOCK_REQ_" + requestId; // Define here for cleanup access
    MDC.put("batchId", batchId);
    // ... [Logic remains identical] ...

    try {
        // ... [Row processing and master insert logic] ...

        // REMOVED: redisTemplate.delete(lockKey) from here
        log.info("BATCH_CREATION_FINISHED | Batch: {} | Duration: {}ms", batchId, System.currentTimeMillis() - start);

    } catch (DataAccessException e) {
        log.error("DATABASE_ERROR | Batch: {} | Status: Persistence failure", batchId);
        handleAsyncFailure(batchId, creatorId, requestId, "Database persistence failed.");
    } catch (Exception e) {
        log.error("SYSTEM_FAULT | Batch: {} | Type: {}", batchId, e.getClass().getSimpleName());
        handleAsyncFailure(batchId, creatorId, requestId, "Internal system error.");
    } finally {
        // Rule 3: Lock cleanup moved to finally block. This ensures that even if a 
        // technical crash occurs, the lock is released immediately so the user can retry.
        redisTemplate.delete(lockKey); 
        redisTemplate.delete("DATA_" + requestId); // Clean up cache as well
        MDC.clear();
    }
}

Why this change is necessary for Production
This ensures Mutual Exclusion. By extending the TTL and moving the deletion to the finally block, you create a "Self-Cleaning" lock.
 * If the process is fast, the lock is deleted immediately.
 * If the process is slow (10+ minutes), the 60-minute TTL prevents a second process from starting.
 * If the server restarts or the thread crashes, the finally block (or eventually the TTL) prevents the Request ID from being permanently "stuck."
